    "blog_text_7BWxfp": {
      "type": "blog-text",
      "custom_css": [
        ".shopify-hardcoded-text-section-content {font-size: 16px; margin-left: 0px; text-align: left;}",
        "@media screen and (max-width: 1024px) {.shopify-hardcoded-text-section-wrapper {min-height: 200px; padding: 16px !important; }}"
      ],
      "name": "Blog Text Section",
      "settings": {
        "text_content": "<p>There is a glass cabinet in quite a lot of British homes. It contains the good china that never gets used, a candle that smells incredible but is too nice to burn, and at least one bottle of designer perfume that gets treated like a controlled substance. You bought it, or someone bought it for you, and now it lives there. Being admired. Not worn. Sprayed approximately twice a year on occasions significant enough to justify it. This is not what it was made for.</p><p><a href=\"https://scentspired.co.uk/\" title=\"https://scentspired.co.uk/\">Scentspired </a>exists because of that bottle. We love fragrance. We also find it quite hard to justify spending £150 on something you end up rationing like medicine. The designers themselves do not have this problem. They have enormous margins, celebrity campaigns, and a very nice shop on Bond Street. What they do not always have is an answer for the person who wants to smell extraordinary on a Wednesday afternoon without remortgaging to do it.</p><p><strong>So we built one.</strong></p>"
      }
    },
